{
  "id" : "8ibc-ffun",
  "name" : "Crosswalk of Career Technical Education (CTE) Programs to Occupations",
  "assetType" : "dataset",
  "attribution" : "Department of Economic Research and Department of Elementary and Secondary Education",
  "averageRating" : 0,
  "category" : "College and Career",
  "createdAt" : 1774560165,
  "description" : "This crosswalk maps Massachusetts' <a href=\"https://www.doe.mass.edu/ccte/pathways/cte/default.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Career Technical Education (CTE) pathway programs</a> to aligned occupations. These are defined as occupations that enrolled students are typically prepared to enter upon graduation given the skills and knowledge covered in the program, without any subsequent schooling.\n\nThe crosswalk was created jointly by the Department of Economic Research (DER) at the Executive Office of Labor and Workforce Development (EOLWD) and the Office of College, Career, and Technical Education at the Department of Elementary and Secondary Education (DESE). It matches active CTE program codes found in the Student Information Management System (SIMS) Data Handbook Appendices with occupations in the Standard Occupational Classification (SOC) system. Mappings were determined based on standard curriculum described in the <a href=\"https://www.doe.mass.edu/ccte/frameworks/frameworks.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CTE Instructional Frameworks</a> as well as occupational requirements observed in recent labor market data. Please note that not all program codes in the SIMS Data Handbook are represented; programs were excluded if no aligned entry-level occupation could be identified, if no graduates had been produced, or if no active programs existed in the state at the time the crosswalk was created.\n\nAdditional versions of this crosswalk will be created as program curriculum or the labor market changes, when there are changes to the SIMS or SOC coding systems, or as currently excluded programs produce graduates, establish active programs, and develop occupational alignments.\n\nData used to create this crosswalk:\n<ul><li>SIMS Data Handbook Appendices v1.1 (2025-2026 school year)</li><li>CTE Instructional Frameworks as of March 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/soc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2018 SOC system</a> from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (detailed 6-digit codes used, except in cases where labor market information was published in 2023 for aggregated codes)</li><li>Labor market data from the American Community Survey 5-year (2018-2023) and Lightcast job postings (2025)</li></ul>For more information on the SOC system or how to use labor market information, please contact economicresearch@mass.gov.",
